Title: Erlotinib-Induced Pneumatosis Intestinalis: A Case Report and Review of Literature.
Abstract: Pneumatosis intestinalis (PI) is a rare condition characterized by gas accumulation within the bowel wall. Although several medications have been associated with the etiopathogenesis of PI, the association between PI and epidermal growth factor receptor (<i>EGFR</i>)-tyrosine kinase inhibitors (TKIs) is less recognized. We present the case of a 79-year-old woman who developed PI 2 months after the initiation of erlotinib. The condition improved after the discontinuation of erlotinib, suggesting a possible association between erlotinib and the development of PI. This case underscores the importance of considering PI in the differential diagnosis when patients receiving <i>EGFR</i>-TKIs present with abdominal symptoms or intramural bowel gas.

